1
00:00:06,492 --> 00:00:09,493
Not my president! Not my president!

2
00:00:09,494 --> 00:00:12,413
- Not my president!
- Not my president!

3
00:00:12,414 --> 00:00:15,575
- Not my president!
- Not my president!

4
00:00:22,173 --> 00:00:23,841
The people are angry.

5
00:00:23,842 --> 00:00:26,552
Nine weeks ago, we had an
election in this country,

6
00:00:26,553 --> 00:00:29,347
but because two states
refused to certify,

7
00:00:29,348 --> 00:00:33,643
neither Conway nor myself
received the 270 electoral votes

8
00:00:33,644 --> 00:00:35,310
required for victory.

9
00:00:35,311 --> 00:00:40,190
So they gather here every morning
to exercise their magnificent right,

10
00:00:40,191 --> 00:00:42,811
guaranteed by the First Amendment...

11
00:00:43,245 --> 00:00:46,898
to let their angry voices be heard.

12
00:00:48,784 --> 00:00:51,629
And so, Thanksgiving, Christmas,

13
00:00:51,630 --> 00:00:54,079
and half of January have come and gone,

14
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:57,207
and all three branches of
government are in crisis.

15
00:00:57,208 --> 00:00:59,001
The Congress is in disarray,

16
00:00:59,002 --> 00:01:01,921
the Supreme Court is down a
justice and doomed to deadlock,

17
00:01:01,922 --> 00:01:05,208
and the executive
branch is without a boss.

18
00:01:06,134 --> 00:01:07,494
You see, my feeling is,

19
00:01:07,495 --> 00:01:10,430
I think the Founding
Fathers, they just got tired.

20
00:01:10,431 --> 00:01:13,724
And really, can you blame them? I
mean, you can't think of everything.

21
00:01:13,725 --> 00:01:15,601
Black swans, Murphy's Law.

22
00:01:15,602 --> 00:01:17,352
I mean, at a certain point,
you just have to sign off

23
00:01:17,353 --> 00:01:20,981
and cross your goddamn
fingers and hope for the best.

24
00:01:20,982 --> 00:01:23,818
Or adopt "flipism,"

25
00:01:23,819 --> 00:01:26,904
a pseudo-philosophy of life in
which the most important decisions

26
00:01:26,905 --> 00:01:29,615
are made by the flipping of a coin.

27
00:01:29,616 --> 00:01:32,452
It was first introduced
in the Disney comic book

28
00:01:32,453 --> 00:01:34,328
Flip Decision, one of my favorites,

29
00:01:34,329 --> 00:01:37,498
in which Donald Duck is
persuaded by Professor Batty

30
00:01:37,499 --> 00:01:39,578
to make all the most important decisions

31
00:01:39,579 --> 00:01:41,502
based on the flipping of a coin.

32
00:01:41,503 --> 00:01:45,999
"Life is but a gamble. Let
flipism guide your ramble."

33
00:01:48,134 --> 00:01:50,219
We've had this sort of trouble before.

34
00:01:50,220 --> 00:01:53,598
The election of 1800,
Jefferson versus Burr.

35
00:01:53,599 --> 00:01:56,434
They wound up in a tie.
73 electoral votes each.

36
00:01:56,435 --> 00:01:59,729
That was the number required
then, and that's when Congress

37
00:01:59,730 --> 00:02:03,649
tried to address what the Founding
Fathers just couldn't imagine.

38
00:02:03,650 --> 00:02:06,944
And, poof, the Twelfth
Amendment was born.

39
00:02:06,945 --> 00:02:10,781
And it says that if the
states don't certify,

40
00:02:10,782 --> 00:02:13,075
it's the House that
chooses the president

41
00:02:13,076 --> 00:02:15,327
and the Senate that
chooses the vice president.

42
00:02:15,328 --> 00:02:16,662
Now the House is a bit of a mess,

43
00:02:16,663 --> 00:02:19,540
but the Senate is rather democratic
in how they choose the VP.

44
00:02:19,541 --> 00:02:21,459
One person, one vote.

45
00:02:21,460 --> 00:02:24,336
But if there's a tie...

46
00:02:24,337 --> 00:02:28,423
and this is where Donald Duck
comes in, it's resolved...

47
00:02:28,424 --> 00:02:30,043
by the toss of a coin.

48
00:02:30,761 --> 00:02:32,553
A solution that's elegant as hell.

49
00:02:32,554 --> 00:02:36,132
But then there's no elegance in
modern politics. It's mostly hell.

50
00:02:38,935 --> 00:02:42,312
The identity of the next
President of these United States

51
00:02:42,313 --> 00:02:45,399
is once again in the hands
of a bunch of self-serving,

52
00:02:45,400 --> 00:02:49,028
money-hungry, boot-licking,
power-seeking politicians

53
00:02:49,029 --> 00:02:53,566
who can be seduced or sucker punched
or blackmailed into submission.

54
00:02:54,535 --> 00:02:57,821
And all I need is just one
more vote than the other guy.

55
00:03:00,123 --> 00:03:03,827
As you can see, the Founding
Fathers didn't plan for this.

56
00:03:05,587 --> 00:03:07,122
But I did.

57
00:03:14,137 --> 00:03:15,839
Meet your new daddy.

58
00:04:49,358 --> 00:04:54,792
<font color="#007700">Synced and corrected by Aaronnmb</font>
www.addic7ed.com

59
00:05:07,208 --> 00:05:08,451
Right.

60
00:05:09,460 --> 00:05:11,621
You can feel it, right?
It's like you're there.

61
00:05:14,716 --> 00:05:16,542
Doesn't that look just like it?

62
00:05:17,260 --> 00:05:19,087
To me, it is that place.

63
00:05:28,813 --> 00:05:30,856
You okay, man?

64
00:05:30,857 --> 00:05:33,317
Huh? Yeah. Of course.

65
00:05:33,318 --> 00:05:36,111
It really helps. Very effective.

66
00:05:36,112 --> 00:05:39,482
Yeah. I'd be happy to help
you find more funding for it.

67
00:05:41,284 --> 00:05:43,535
If you're feeling a little worked up...

68
00:05:43,536 --> 00:05:44,779
What?

69
00:05:46,790 --> 00:05:48,408
Little worked up?

70
00:05:49,042 --> 00:05:52,837
I was elected President of the
United States over two months ago.

71
00:05:52,838 --> 00:05:55,422
But somehow, Congress is
now deciding the election.

72
00:05:55,423 --> 00:05:57,382
So forgive me for being a little...

73
00:05:57,383 --> 00:05:58,751
- Will...
- ... worked up.

74
00:05:59,469 --> 00:06:01,879
I don't think Tony
was implying anything.

75
00:06:02,889 --> 00:06:04,757
Of course. Good.

76
00:06:05,851 --> 00:06:07,768
I'm gonna get myself ready.

77
00:06:07,769 --> 00:06:09,603
I don't want to go to the
event looking like this.

78
00:06:09,604 --> 00:06:11,222
Right.

79
00:06:12,357 --> 00:06:14,017
Thanks, Tony.

80
00:06:21,116 --> 00:06:23,743
Romero's chief of staff
insists he's not in town.

81
00:06:23,744 --> 00:06:25,619
- And do you believe her?
- No.

82
00:06:25,620 --> 00:06:27,955
But she's been in D.C.
longer than he has.

83
00:06:27,956 --> 00:06:29,539
At some point she'll
take care of herself.

84
00:06:29,540 --> 00:06:31,375
Well, did you tell her I
have no interest in discussing

85
00:06:31,376 --> 00:06:33,543
the Declaration of
War Committee with him?

86
00:06:33,544 --> 00:06:34,779
I expressed our desire

87
00:06:34,780 --> 00:06:36,839
for a civil and productive
meeting with the Congressman.

88
00:06:36,840 --> 00:06:39,842
Well, find him. If he wants to hide
from me, he can do it to my face.

89
00:06:39,843 --> 00:06:41,343
Yes, sir.

90
00:06:41,344 --> 00:06:43,296
Oh, and, Doug.

91
00:06:45,807 --> 00:06:48,093
How many protestors are out there today?

92
00:06:48,560 --> 00:06:50,477
More than yesterday.

93
00:06:50,478 --> 00:06:53,431
But they're doing their best to
divert them away from the White House.

94
00:06:54,607 --> 00:06:56,483
I don't know what's worse...

95
00:06:56,484 --> 00:07:01,064
the sound of their chanting or the
sound of silence when they're not.

96
00:07:03,825 --> 00:07:05,360
I'll get to work, sir.

97
00:07:31,227 --> 00:07:32,845
Good morning, Augustus.

98
00:07:33,396 --> 00:07:36,057
Well, Eric, I...

99
00:07:36,775 --> 00:07:38,901
I'm sorry. They told
me to sit, but, uh...

100
00:07:38,902 --> 00:07:41,486
Oh, no, no, no. It's
perfectly fine. I...

101
00:07:41,487 --> 00:07:43,982
I can't imagine anyone else who would...

102
00:07:44,991 --> 00:07:48,577
well, enjoy this more than you.

103
00:07:48,578 --> 00:07:54,083
You know, I only started it after
you and I met in Spotsylvania.

104
00:07:54,084 --> 00:07:57,211
The attention to detail
is... it's just...

105
00:07:57,212 --> 00:07:58,663
It is, yes.

106
00:08:02,133 --> 00:08:03,751
I've got...

107
00:08:04,552 --> 00:08:05,920
I mean, I...

108
00:08:07,472 --> 00:08:12,226
I'm sorry, I'm a... a little
overwhelmed by the moment.

109
00:08:12,227 --> 00:08:13,928
It's just...

110
00:08:14,395 --> 00:08:17,606
I have this feeling, as if, uh...

111
00:08:17,607 --> 00:08:20,393
I've imagined this somehow.

112
00:08:21,319 --> 00:08:23,604
I know that sounds, uh...

113
00:08:27,075 --> 00:08:30,244
Uh, you've even got the
button on his haversack.

114
00:08:30,245 --> 00:08:31,529
Yes.

115
00:08:32,413 --> 00:08:35,408
I think people underestimate...

116
00:08:37,043 --> 00:08:39,995
the importance of detail...

117
00:08:40,713 --> 00:08:42,998
to the overall impact.

118
00:08:47,053 --> 00:08:48,379
Yeah.

119
00:08:49,222 --> 00:08:52,258
The Bloody Angle experts
would be very impressed.

120
00:08:53,018 --> 00:08:57,347
When we met in Spotsylvania,
how did you know...

121
00:08:58,064 --> 00:09:02,109
so many specific things
about Augustus, uh...

122
00:09:02,110 --> 00:09:05,905
about Augustus Underwood's death?
I mean, the very spot where he died?

123
00:09:05,906 --> 00:09:08,782
The... the fact that his skull
had been crushed with a rock?

124
00:09:08,783 --> 00:09:12,445
That they buried him in an
unmarked grave? I mean...

125
00:09:12,954 --> 00:09:15,281
the detail of all that.

126
00:09:16,666 --> 00:09:18,618
Hope you'll forgive me, uh...

127
00:09:19,335 --> 00:09:20,536
For?

128
00:09:21,421 --> 00:09:24,673
I was meeting Vice
President Francis Underwood.

129
00:09:24,674 --> 00:09:28,169
I... thought he deserved a good story.

130
00:09:32,473 --> 00:09:35,843
Oh... wait, wait a minute. You...

131
00:09:38,021 --> 00:09:40,598
- you made all that up?
- Well...

132
00:09:41,524 --> 00:09:44,477
I took some liberties.

133
00:09:47,822 --> 00:09:51,359
But, well, I don't know, once it
started, it came to me so clearly...

134
00:09:57,540 --> 00:09:59,700
- It's Eric, yeah?
- Yes, sir.

135
00:10:08,634 --> 00:10:10,170
The crowd?

136
00:10:13,139 --> 00:10:16,842
Yes. I'm sorry, what
were you about to say?

137
00:10:20,021 --> 00:10:22,473
I don't mean to overstep, sir...

138
00:10:23,066 --> 00:10:25,943
but the people out there...

139
00:10:25,944 --> 00:10:27,812
I don't care what they're saying.

140
00:10:28,529 --> 00:10:31,774
And the way I see it, a
person's destiny is, you know...

141
00:10:32,783 --> 00:10:34,068
sealed.

142
00:10:35,286 --> 00:10:37,079
We are in uncharted territory here.

143
00:10:37,080 --> 00:10:39,498
All right. But, Donny,
do me a favor, okay?

144
00:10:39,499 --> 00:10:41,083
- Let the Constitution...
- Can I finish?

145
00:10:41,084 --> 00:10:43,585
Hold on. Just let the
Constitution do its job.

146
00:10:43,586 --> 00:10:45,003
Okay, Joe, Joe, I'm
gonna finish for once.

147
00:10:45,004 --> 00:10:47,805
You've got voters filing
individual suits...

148
00:10:47,806 --> 00:10:49,204
- Right. That's fine.
- ... in Tennessee and Ohio,

149
00:10:49,205 --> 00:10:50,689
and they are jammed up
in the Supreme Court.

150
00:10:50,690 --> 00:10:51,821
Like everything else,

151
00:10:51,822 --> 00:10:54,573
the day-to-day workings of
this country are on hold.

152
00:10:54,574 --> 00:10:57,391
Okay, they may be on hold, but
guess what? Last time I checked,

153
00:10:57,392 --> 00:10:59,957
everybody was still getting
their Social Security checks.

154
00:10:59,958 --> 00:11:01,082
Yeah, but if the debt limit is reached?

155
00:11:01,083 --> 00:11:02,896
Oh, come on. Let me finish now.

156
00:11:02,897 --> 00:11:05,399
Again, it's why we have a Constitution.

157
00:11:05,400 --> 00:11:09,945
I know it looks messy.
Democracy's messy. But guess what?

158
00:11:09,946 --> 00:11:12,406
There is a method to that madness.

159
00:11:12,407 --> 00:11:14,658
I don't know, Joe. It's more
madness than method right now.

160
00:11:14,659 --> 00:11:16,159
I mean, if the
government does shut down,

161
00:11:16,160 --> 00:11:18,215
those Social Security
checks, they might not go out.

162
00:11:18,216 --> 00:11:19,442
If the government shuts down,

163
00:11:19,443 --> 00:11:21,915
who knows if Congress
can even pick a president?

164
00:11:21,916 --> 00:11:24,084
Each state in the House
just gets one vote.

165
00:11:24,085 --> 00:11:26,878
So if smaller states like
Delaware and Rhode Island

166
00:11:26,879 --> 00:11:28,422
can't reach a consensus...

167
00:11:28,423 --> 00:11:30,632
- Right.
- No, it's true. I mean, if one or two

168
00:11:30,633 --> 00:11:32,843
of these really stubborn
members of Congress

169
00:11:32,844 --> 00:11:34,594
decides they're going to hold
this up, it could be months.

170
00:11:34,595 --> 00:11:37,681
Look, that's what I'm saying. The
road to 26 votes is not an easy one.

171
00:11:37,682 --> 00:11:40,968
All right. Strange days indeed, Mika.

172
00:11:45,690 --> 00:11:48,309
So are these the start to short stories?

173
00:11:49,026 --> 00:11:50,270
Mmm-hmm.

174
00:11:51,071 --> 00:11:53,731
They're nothing yet. Fragments.

175
00:11:54,782 --> 00:11:56,191
Pieces.

176
00:11:58,828 --> 00:12:00,829
I mean, clearly, this is about me.

177
00:12:00,830 --> 00:12:03,039
- Mmm-hmm.
- Uh, this is my mother.

178
00:12:03,040 --> 00:12:06,118
- Mmm-hmm.
- And this is us.

179
00:12:07,545 --> 00:12:09,622
- You can't write...
- I'm writing.

180
00:12:10,881 --> 00:12:13,049
It's what I do. I'm a writer.

181
00:12:13,050 --> 00:12:15,802
- I know you're frustrated, Tom, but...
- I wouldn't say frustrated.

182
00:12:15,803 --> 00:12:17,505
Well, it's invasive.

183
00:12:19,599 --> 00:12:22,601
- Write about your own experiences.
- This is my experience.

184
00:12:22,602 --> 00:12:24,018
Listen, Tom,

185
00:12:24,019 --> 00:12:26,521
just because we've decided
Francis and I need to stay here,

186
00:12:26,522 --> 00:12:27,606
doesn't mean you have to.

187
00:12:27,607 --> 00:12:30,275
So if you have things you need to
do, you should go ahead and do them.

188
00:12:30,276 --> 00:12:33,445
I'll be spending my day convincing
the Senate to vote for me,

189
00:12:33,446 --> 00:12:35,398
so there's no speeches necessary.

190
00:12:36,407 --> 00:12:37,991
Yeah, okay.

191
00:12:37,992 --> 00:12:40,327
You know what? I do
have some things to do.

192
00:12:40,328 --> 00:12:42,746
Some people I need to
check in with. So...

193
00:12:42,747 --> 00:12:44,289
Tom.

194
00:12:44,290 --> 00:12:46,916
Yeah?

195
00:12:46,917 --> 00:12:48,084
Yes?

196
00:12:48,085 --> 00:12:50,830
You ready, Mrs. Underwood?

197
00:12:56,511 --> 00:12:58,421
I'll have a car waiting for you.

198
00:13:07,480 --> 00:13:10,190
So do you want the good
news or the bad news first?

199
00:13:10,191 --> 00:13:11,726
Always the bad.

200
00:13:12,443 --> 00:13:15,020
The president's
approvals are down to 19%.

201
00:13:16,197 --> 00:13:17,406
And the good?

202
00:13:17,407 --> 00:13:20,443
They're worse for
Congress. And you're at 30.

203
00:13:22,203 --> 00:13:26,028
Well, thankfully it's Congress we
have to convince now, not the public.

204
00:13:26,624 --> 00:13:29,168
These are the remaining senators
we'll target before the vote.

205
00:13:29,169 --> 00:13:30,419
All women, by the way.

206
00:13:30,420 --> 00:13:34,798
The biggest challenges will be
Indiana's freshman senator Susan Newman.

207
00:13:34,799 --> 00:13:36,758
Mmm. The constitutional lawyer?

208
00:13:36,759 --> 00:13:38,802
Uh-huh. And Hawaii's Kerry Kuilima,

209
00:13:38,803 --> 00:13:42,172
who seems to be enjoying getting
to wield a little power for once.

210
00:13:43,641 --> 00:13:47,269
I need to tell you, there is
a lot of push-back on the Hill

211
00:13:47,270 --> 00:13:49,930
about the simultaneous
vote for president and VP.

212
00:13:50,439 --> 00:13:52,607
The country is terrified
it'll lead to a split ticket.

213
00:13:52,608 --> 00:13:54,776
There's threats of a filibuster.

214
00:13:54,777 --> 00:13:56,145
Well, the Dems won the Senate,

215
00:13:56,146 --> 00:13:58,322
and they want to vote
when the House does.

216
00:13:58,323 --> 00:14:00,508
That decision's been made.

217
00:14:00,991 --> 00:14:04,494
And the vice president won't
allow a filibuster of any kind

218
00:14:04,495 --> 00:14:06,288
onto the floor.

219
00:14:06,289 --> 00:14:08,957
Ma'am, have you contemplated
how it could possibly work,

220
00:14:08,958 --> 00:14:11,160
you as vice president
to President Conway?

221
00:14:12,253 --> 00:14:13,871
That's not going to happen.

222
00:14:17,342 --> 00:14:18,751
Of course.

223
00:14:23,431 --> 00:14:26,600
I did learn one true thing
about Augustus Underwood.

224
00:14:26,601 --> 00:14:28,093
What?

225
00:14:29,312 --> 00:14:31,639
He was dead. Cold.

226
00:14:32,565 --> 00:14:36,226
They put him on a cart...
bunch of dead bodies to bury.

227
00:14:37,027 --> 00:14:39,564
Then, after a full day and night...

228
00:14:41,657 --> 00:14:43,150
he woke up.

229
00:14:44,452 --> 00:14:45,535
What?

230
00:14:45,536 --> 00:14:49,990
Someone stole his shoes and the
buttons off his uniform, but...

231
00:14:51,083 --> 00:14:52,577
he lived.

232
00:14:53,794 --> 00:14:55,955
Few days later, he
caught up to his regiment.

233
00:14:58,758 --> 00:15:00,084
Really?

234
00:15:07,182 --> 00:15:10,727
I have to get back to the madness.

235
00:15:10,728 --> 00:15:13,347
I hope I get to see you
again, Mr. President.

236
00:15:23,324 --> 00:15:28,995
Eric... what do you do when
you're not, uh... reenacting?

237
00:15:28,996 --> 00:15:30,747
I'm an actor.

238
00:15:30,748 --> 00:15:33,283
Uh, sometimes. I mean, I'd do a
lot more if someone would let me.

239
00:15:34,419 --> 00:15:37,296
So, right now, I'm
mostly a personal trainer.

240
00:15:37,297 --> 00:15:39,749
- A personal trainer?
- Yeah.

241
00:15:42,802 --> 00:15:46,839
- You know where to find me.
- Uh, and, sir...

242
00:15:48,140 --> 00:15:50,843
no matter how dire things look...

243
00:15:51,727 --> 00:15:53,804
you're gonna come out of this alive.

244
00:15:56,231 --> 00:15:57,683
You're an Underwood, after all.

245
00:16:06,033 --> 00:16:08,868
No, don't... don't
"chief-of-staff" me, Eleanor.

246
00:16:08,869 --> 00:16:11,455
No, don't give me his
schedule. I don't want to hear

247
00:16:11,456 --> 00:16:14,249
about how Congressman Romero
is meeting with his constituents

248
00:16:14,250 --> 00:16:16,577
or stuck on an Acela train.

249
00:16:19,004 --> 00:16:22,674
You know, it's Fazio,
right? The pronunciation?

250
00:16:22,675 --> 00:16:24,509
Eleanor Fazio?

251
00:16:24,510 --> 00:16:26,219
I... I just want to be sure.

252
00:16:26,220 --> 00:16:28,763
Because when the president
wins, and he is going to win,

253
00:16:28,764 --> 00:16:30,550
I will remember your name.

254
00:16:32,727 --> 00:16:34,811
I still need you to review this release.

255
00:16:34,812 --> 00:16:37,731
Now, obviously, the goal is to
combat the "hiding out" narrative,

256
00:16:37,732 --> 00:16:39,767
emphasizing the work that they're doing.

257
00:16:41,569 --> 00:16:45,530
- Is it a personal matter?
- Business. It's always business.

258
00:16:45,531 --> 00:16:47,281
I'll e-mail you my sign off.

259
00:16:47,282 --> 00:16:50,827
Wait a second. I need an update
on their schedule for tomorrow.

260
00:16:50,828 --> 00:16:53,461
- We're almost there.
- I want to know how you found out.

261
00:16:53,462 --> 00:16:54,546
If he needs help,

262
00:16:54,547 --> 00:16:56,171
that can happen after he
makes it to the White House.

263
00:16:56,172 --> 00:16:57,833
Mark, you're not answering my question.

264
00:16:58,550 --> 00:17:00,635
If word of treatment, any
kind of treatment, got out...

265
00:17:00,636 --> 00:17:02,762
His friend was showing
him the therapy. That's it.

266
00:17:02,763 --> 00:17:06,466
- Please don't insult me.
- Who do you think you are?

267
00:17:06,467 --> 00:17:09,018
Inserting yourself into
every aspect of our lives.

268
00:17:09,019 --> 00:17:10,815
Your husband's campaign needed help.

269
00:17:10,816 --> 00:17:12,009
- Help arrived.
- Yes, but...

270
00:17:12,010 --> 00:17:13,814
Help that you, most of all, pushed for.

271
00:17:13,815 --> 00:17:15,566
Yes?

272
00:17:15,567 --> 00:17:18,694
- Who told you about the VR therapy?
- He did.

273
00:17:20,030 --> 00:17:21,531
Let's go.

274
00:17:21,532 --> 00:17:22,941
What's going on?

275
00:17:24,368 --> 00:17:27,578
I was just telling Mark how much
I miss having Marshall around.

276
00:17:27,579 --> 00:17:28,864
Well, he's still around.

277
00:17:36,046 --> 00:17:37,547
Where are you going?

278
00:17:37,548 --> 00:17:39,338
I forgot my bag.

279
00:17:39,339 --> 00:17:41,041
It's right here.

280
00:17:43,011 --> 00:17:44,178
Hello. Yeah.

281
00:17:44,179 --> 00:17:46,089
Yes, sir, he's right here.

282
00:17:47,766 --> 00:17:49,801
- It's him.
- Ah.

283
00:17:52,187 --> 00:17:53,597
Congressman.

284
00:17:55,407 --> 00:17:56,838
You have no idea how bad this could get.

285
00:17:56,839 --> 00:17:58,025
We don't wanna be late.

286
00:17:58,026 --> 00:18:00,771
Well, I couldn't agree more.

287
00:18:07,744 --> 00:18:09,696
She might have gone to Santa Fe.

288
00:18:15,336 --> 00:18:16,828
You don't believe me, do you?

289
00:18:18,004 --> 00:18:20,715
I would need to see something,
Lisa. Something concrete.

290
00:18:20,716 --> 00:18:23,627
I have a photo. I have one, but...

291
00:18:26,012 --> 00:18:27,338
Listen.

292
00:18:29,015 --> 00:18:30,216
Look at me.

293
00:18:31,310 --> 00:18:33,894
You're not the first person to
come here making false claims

294
00:18:33,895 --> 00:18:36,021
- about Underwood and his cronies.
- But I'm telling the truth.

295
00:18:36,022 --> 00:18:37,982
Everything you're saying you
could've read in the paper.

296
00:18:37,983 --> 00:18:40,526
That's how I realized who he
was. His picture in the paper.

297
00:18:40,527 --> 00:18:43,863
Which makes your story harder to
prove. We're talking in circles.

298
00:18:43,864 --> 00:18:46,281
What about Doug Stamper driving
me home when my car broke down,

299
00:18:46,282 --> 00:18:49,243
telling me his name was Peter
and that he sold insurance?

300
00:18:49,244 --> 00:18:51,404
- No one can corroborate that story.
- Rachel!

301
00:18:52,748 --> 00:18:54,199
Rachel could.

302
00:18:59,671 --> 00:19:02,089
- You should finish that.
- I'm not hungry.

303
00:19:02,090 --> 00:19:03,959
Maybe I am. I don't know.

304
00:19:05,386 --> 00:19:07,754
I want to thank you for reaching out.

305
00:19:13,644 --> 00:19:17,229
I am not saying I don't believe you.

306
00:19:17,230 --> 00:19:19,535
But there's already
enough evidence out there

307
00:19:19,536 --> 00:19:21,066
to take the president down.

308
00:19:21,067 --> 00:19:23,694
And now, with this election,
he'll be accountable for even more.

309
00:19:23,695 --> 00:19:25,981
- I'm certain of it.
- I don't care.

310
00:19:27,073 --> 00:19:31,160
I don't care about Underwood. I
want you to talk to Doug Stamper.

311
00:19:31,161 --> 00:19:33,529
I want you to find out
what happened to my...

312
00:19:34,540 --> 00:19:36,749
Whatever, forget it.

313
00:19:36,750 --> 00:19:39,327
- Uh. Hold on.
- Thanks for the sandwich.

314
00:19:42,464 --> 00:19:44,207
All right. Hold on.

315
00:19:55,394 --> 00:19:57,770
There's a story that I love about LBJ.

316
00:19:57,771 --> 00:20:01,023
Uh, it was back in '64 or '65.

317
00:20:01,024 --> 00:20:04,735
A congressman was ducking him...
and he came back to his office

318
00:20:04,736 --> 00:20:07,655
to find the president
sitting in his chair...

319
00:20:07,656 --> 00:20:08,982
waiting for him.

320
00:20:11,868 --> 00:20:14,445
The president expects to
see you within the hour.

321
00:20:15,622 --> 00:20:19,034
Miss Fazio can give you the specifics.

322
00:20:20,210 --> 00:20:23,664
- He was wearing his cowboy hat.
- What?

323
00:20:25,215 --> 00:20:28,877
President Johnson. You left
out the best part of the story.

324
00:20:50,115 --> 00:20:53,121
We closed the place down last
night. Thanks for the invite.

325
00:20:53,122 --> 00:20:54,866
Bunch of us are headed back
to the same spot tonight,

326
00:20:54,867 --> 00:20:56,559
- if you wanna come.
- Cool.

327
00:20:56,560 --> 00:20:59,499
Uh, he has a late lunch
today. Won't be back till 4:00.

328
00:20:59,500 --> 00:21:01,484
Oh, I just needed to drop this for him.

329
00:21:01,485 --> 00:21:03,002
Sure.

330
00:21:03,003 --> 00:21:04,663
Next time, just ask.

331
00:21:05,380 --> 00:21:06,790
See you tonight.

332
00:21:16,433 --> 00:21:18,934
You gambled and you lost.

333
00:21:18,935 --> 00:21:22,480
Actually, my bloc gained seats
more than eight weeks ago.

334
00:21:22,481 --> 00:21:25,983
- Which I suspect is why I'm here.
- Don't overestimate your position.

335
00:21:25,984 --> 00:21:27,902
Or underestimate mine.

336
00:21:27,903 --> 00:21:29,662
You still haven't been
elected president, sir.

337
00:21:29,663 --> 00:21:31,279
Yeah, but the presidency won't matter

338
00:21:31,280 --> 00:21:34,408
once the party turns
its back on you, will it?

339
00:21:34,409 --> 00:21:38,954
When Bob Birch puts all the
rest of your sheep in line,

340
00:21:38,955 --> 00:21:41,908
you'll be begging to
come in from the cold.

341
00:21:42,292 --> 00:21:44,710
And two years from now,
you'll be back in Phoenix

342
00:21:44,711 --> 00:21:48,255
replaying this very moment
over and over again in your mind

343
00:21:48,256 --> 00:21:51,133
and realizing it was
the beginning of the end.

344
00:21:51,134 --> 00:21:53,594
Patricia Whittaker and
the Democratic Party

345
00:21:53,595 --> 00:21:55,304
will wipe their hands of you.

346
00:21:55,305 --> 00:21:57,431
- I'm loyal to the party.
- As you should be.

347
00:21:57,432 --> 00:21:59,217
As you should say.

348
00:22:00,143 --> 00:22:01,594
But really...

349
00:22:02,854 --> 00:22:04,765
what's your leverage?

350
00:22:05,982 --> 00:22:10,069
The committee? That's done. You
see, you've already put out, Alex,

351
00:22:10,070 --> 00:22:12,404
and Will Conway is not
taking you to the prom.

352
00:22:12,405 --> 00:22:14,054
My conversations with the governor

353
00:22:14,055 --> 00:22:16,033
and any position that
I may or may not have...

354
00:22:16,034 --> 00:22:18,630
Oh, listen to yourself.
Do you even believe you?

355
00:22:18,631 --> 00:22:20,116
You are awfully confident

356
00:22:20,117 --> 00:22:22,180
for someone who's already
lost the election once.

357
00:22:24,292 --> 00:22:26,794
Do me a favor. Forget the
presidency for just a moment.

358
00:22:26,795 --> 00:22:28,371
You don't have...

359
00:22:29,419 --> 00:22:31,710
the capital... to build

360
00:22:31,711 --> 00:22:34,717
on the congressional
revolution that you've started,

361
00:22:34,718 --> 00:22:35,835
politically or otherwise.

362
00:22:35,836 --> 00:22:37,221
Our movement wants to explode things...

363
00:22:37,222 --> 00:22:41,174
Oh, please. Spare me your
progressive PR bullshit.

364
00:22:42,978 --> 00:22:44,387
You want what they all want.

365
00:22:45,480 --> 00:22:49,017
Access. A better seat at the table.

366
00:22:49,818 --> 00:22:51,769
Your name before the rest.

367
00:22:53,655 --> 00:22:56,741
And that is something
that I can give you.

368
00:22:56,742 --> 00:22:58,743
And how many votes do
you want in exchange?

369
00:22:58,744 --> 00:23:01,453
Just three from your ever-growing bloc

370
00:23:01,454 --> 00:23:04,539
would assure me the
win in key delegations.

371
00:23:04,540 --> 00:23:06,208
Specifically?

372
00:23:06,209 --> 00:23:09,537
Maine, Wisconsin and New
Hampshire are still in play.

373
00:23:10,881 --> 00:23:12,082
All three?

374
00:23:17,470 --> 00:23:20,014
I believe in you, Congressman.
Do you wanna know why?

375
00:23:20,015 --> 00:23:21,216
Tell me.

376
00:23:22,017 --> 00:23:25,386
Because I trust ambition.

377
00:23:26,396 --> 00:23:30,149
- So this is a straight-up bribe.
- Oh, no, Congressman.

378
00:23:30,150 --> 00:23:32,894
A bribe is something you can refuse.

379
00:23:35,864 --> 00:23:39,449
Now, since you haven't been around
the block as many times as I have,

380
00:23:39,450 --> 00:23:40,951
let me walk you through how this works.

381
00:23:40,952 --> 00:23:43,620
Excuse me, Mr. President, but
I do know how this is done.

382
00:23:43,621 --> 00:23:45,281
Oh, well, that's good to hear.

383
00:23:47,417 --> 00:23:49,870
I'll start with Maine. She owes me.

384
00:23:52,380 --> 00:23:55,090
You know, we did meet
once before, Mr. President.

385
00:23:55,091 --> 00:23:57,592
At a dinner, during the 2012 convention.

386
00:23:57,593 --> 00:24:00,588
- I tried to introduce myself to you...
- And?

387
00:24:02,348 --> 00:24:04,968
- You don't remember, do you?
- Why would I?

388
00:24:08,354 --> 00:24:11,648
Why is the moderator so
interested in Brockhart's opinion?

389
00:24:11,649 --> 00:24:14,234
Has everyone suddenly forgotten
now I'm at the top of the ticket?

390
00:24:14,235 --> 00:24:15,686
No, of course not.

391
00:24:17,030 --> 00:24:18,864
Are we done here?

392
00:24:18,865 --> 00:24:21,567
Yeah. We should talk
about tomorrow's schedule.

393
00:24:22,285 --> 00:24:25,412
Uh, vote's looking good now.

394
00:24:25,413 --> 00:24:28,373
We still need to talk to as
many congressmen as we can.

395
00:24:28,374 --> 00:24:30,125
Well, you figure it
out and give us a call.

396
00:24:30,126 --> 00:24:32,837
And, uh, enough with the events
targeting the Armed Services.

397
00:24:32,838 --> 00:24:35,297
We got as many Congress people
as we could get out of this.

398
00:24:35,298 --> 00:24:38,926
No offense, General, but we
need to divide and conquer. Okay?

399
00:24:38,927 --> 00:24:41,511
Will, I was speaking with Mark...

400
00:24:41,512 --> 00:24:44,306
- Yeah.
- ... and I want you to know,

401
00:24:44,307 --> 00:24:47,218
if you need to talk about
anything, I'm available.

402
00:24:54,359 --> 00:24:56,394
The only support I need, General...

403
00:24:57,112 --> 00:25:00,190
is that of a vice president
to his president. Okay?

404
00:25:10,959 --> 00:25:13,919
Senator Talbert's priorities
are affordable housing

405
00:25:13,920 --> 00:25:16,881
and closing corporate tax loopholes.

406
00:25:16,882 --> 00:25:19,466
And access to higher
education especially.

407
00:25:19,467 --> 00:25:21,635
- The call is in ten.
- Mmm.

408
00:25:21,636 --> 00:25:23,921
Why did Aidan Macallan run away?

409
00:25:26,016 --> 00:25:27,641
What?

410
00:25:27,642 --> 00:25:30,553
Why did Macallan run away?

411
00:25:30,937 --> 00:25:32,555
I wish I could tell you.

412
00:25:34,941 --> 00:25:36,392
Clock's ticking.

413
00:25:45,160 --> 00:25:48,071
Yes, give me Tom Yates'
driver's number, please.

414
00:26:01,843 --> 00:26:03,044
Yes.

415
00:26:04,512 --> 00:26:05,755
Yes, ma'am.

416
00:26:06,848 --> 00:26:07,932
New York.

417
00:26:07,933 --> 00:26:10,725
You drove all the way to New York?

418
00:26:10,726 --> 00:26:12,012
Where?

419
00:26:14,230 --> 00:26:15,355
Who's he with?

420
00:26:15,356 --> 00:26:18,851
I don't know. A woman. Older.

421
00:26:21,571 --> 00:26:23,439
I don't know how that'll work, ma'am.

422
00:26:24,365 --> 00:26:27,110
Yes. Yes, I'll try.

423
00:26:36,002 --> 00:26:38,496
No, I understand your concern, Senator.

424
00:26:40,506 --> 00:26:42,757
Student loan reform
is a priority for you,

425
00:26:42,758 --> 00:26:45,503
so it will be a priority
for the president and me.

426
00:26:46,512 --> 00:26:49,340
Yes. Yes, I do.

427
00:26:52,435 --> 00:26:54,895
Well, Louise, you as
a pillar of the party,

428
00:26:54,896 --> 00:26:58,232
it will be much appreciated if
I can get your help tomorrow.

429
00:26:58,233 --> 00:27:01,018
And it'll just be you and
me and a couple of your...

430
00:27:02,195 --> 00:27:04,355
female colleagues in the Senate.

431
00:27:06,616 --> 00:27:09,360
Okay, well, I look forward
to a productive dialogue.

432
00:27:31,975 --> 00:27:35,303
- Better late than never.
- Sorry I missed dinner.

433
00:27:40,858 --> 00:27:42,692
Why the back?

434
00:27:42,693 --> 00:27:45,487
I'm pretending you're an
Uber. Just help me out.

435
00:27:45,488 --> 00:27:48,649
My sister-in-law. She
came when you couldn't.

436
00:28:16,227 --> 00:28:18,554
Do you think the dead judge us?

437
00:28:27,948 --> 00:28:29,149
No.

438
00:29:18,498 --> 00:29:20,825
What did he do that you liked the most?

439
00:29:22,668 --> 00:29:25,955
Here... in bed.

440
00:29:34,722 --> 00:29:36,216
Sitting up was the best.

441
00:29:38,893 --> 00:29:40,886
Legs around each other.

442
00:29:43,564 --> 00:29:46,518
He'd run his right hand
up the back of my neck

443
00:29:46,519 --> 00:29:48,735
into my hair and pull it.

444
00:29:48,736 --> 00:29:51,021
My hair and my head back...

445
00:29:55,076 --> 00:29:56,361
And?

446
00:30:01,624 --> 00:30:04,452
And with the other hand,
he'd pull me in and out.

447
00:30:07,672 --> 00:30:09,790
Always looking at me.

448
00:30:12,302 --> 00:30:13,669
Seeing me.

449
00:30:20,143 --> 00:30:21,844
You want me to do that?

450
00:30:26,691 --> 00:30:27,975
Yeah.

451
00:30:40,121 --> 00:30:43,157
It's so nice in here.
You can hardly hear them.

452
00:30:43,874 --> 00:30:46,076
Oh, trust me, I can hear them.

453
00:30:47,920 --> 00:30:49,747
With New Hampshire, it's what?

454
00:30:50,756 --> 00:30:53,467
Twenty-four. With Maine, 25.

455
00:30:53,468 --> 00:30:57,512
- Twenty-six if we get Wisconsin.
- Where's Tom?

456
00:30:57,513 --> 00:30:59,598
He needed to get some air.

457
00:30:59,599 --> 00:31:02,927
- Wisconsin is being stubborn.
- Then who else?

458
00:31:03,978 --> 00:31:08,391
Iowa, maybe Colorado, 24, 25.

459
00:31:10,110 --> 00:31:13,438
How was your
great-great-great-grandfather Augustus?

460
00:31:14,155 --> 00:31:15,981
He goes by Eric.

461
00:31:17,658 --> 00:31:18,943
Eric.

462
00:31:20,078 --> 00:31:22,738
He admired my craftsmanship.

463
00:31:24,457 --> 00:31:26,575
We should make a play for North Dakota.

464
00:31:26,576 --> 00:31:28,042
Agreed.

465
00:31:28,043 --> 00:31:31,706
In the Senate, I think you can
swing some votes from Missouri.

466
00:31:32,632 --> 00:31:35,049
Did you ask LeAnn about Macallan?

467
00:31:35,050 --> 00:31:37,587
She didn't know anything. I believe her.

468
00:31:39,555 --> 00:31:41,382
We have to quit again.

469
00:31:43,601 --> 00:31:45,928
Otherwise, they'll kill us.

470
00:31:55,530 --> 00:31:59,984
No Macallan. He was there,
though. Left his belongings.

471
00:32:01,619 --> 00:32:03,162
Anything from the National Police?

472
00:32:03,163 --> 00:32:08,082
No. CIA's man in the palace isn't
even sure he's still in Jakarta.

473
00:32:08,083 --> 00:32:10,585
They'll find him, LeAnn.
That's all I can give you.

474
00:32:10,586 --> 00:32:11,829
I appreciate your help.

475
00:32:17,635 --> 00:32:18,968
Kerry, Susan, what can I get you?

476
00:32:18,969 --> 00:32:20,595
- I'm fine, thanks.
- I'm good with water.

477
00:32:20,596 --> 00:32:23,298
- What about you, Louise?
- What are you having?

478
00:32:24,309 --> 00:32:28,220
- Splash of vodka on ice.
- Make it two.

479
00:32:29,772 --> 00:32:32,065
Susan and I were talking
on the way over, Claire.

480
00:32:32,066 --> 00:32:35,777
This election mess has left us all
facing some scary possibilities.

481
00:32:35,778 --> 00:32:38,530
- Conway wins in the House...
- Which is far from inevitable.

482
00:32:38,531 --> 00:32:40,824
But if a split ticket were to happen...

483
00:32:40,825 --> 00:32:42,617
it would be bad for this country.

484
00:32:42,618 --> 00:32:45,488
No, Conway is what would
be bad for this country.

485
00:32:46,247 --> 00:32:50,417
Let's hope the House does the right
thing. But as far as the Senate,

486
00:32:50,418 --> 00:32:53,295
I am very grateful that we have
Donald Blythe steering that ship.

487
00:32:53,296 --> 00:32:55,481
Mrs. Underwood, I'm sorry to say this,

488
00:32:55,482 --> 00:32:58,091
but no matter what you're offering here,

489
00:32:58,092 --> 00:33:03,722
I can't support the president. And,
by extension, I can't support you.

490
00:33:03,723 --> 00:33:06,008
It's not you, Claire. It's him.

491
00:33:06,476 --> 00:33:09,353
Susan and I are on the
same page about this.

492
00:33:09,354 --> 00:33:12,640
What if this isn't about
Francis Underwood, huh?

493
00:33:13,023 --> 00:33:16,894
It's Claire Underwood who
has the promising future.

494
00:33:18,446 --> 00:33:21,323
I want a woman in the White House.

495
00:33:21,324 --> 00:33:24,409
And if she has to start
as a vice president, fine.

496
00:33:24,410 --> 00:33:26,531
But it's time.

497
00:33:27,247 --> 00:33:28,770
It's time.

498
00:33:28,771 --> 00:33:32,626
I hear that, Louise, but I'll say it...

499
00:33:32,627 --> 00:33:34,919
a vote for you is a
vote for Frank Underwood.

500
00:33:34,920 --> 00:33:39,625
Listen, I understand your concerns
about Francis' record. I do.

501
00:33:40,593 --> 00:33:42,802
If Conway wins,

502
00:33:42,803 --> 00:33:46,006
wouldn't you want a Democrat
to keep him in check?

503
00:33:47,933 --> 00:33:52,387
And what if a vote for me isn't
necessarily a vote for Francis?

504
00:33:54,565 --> 00:33:56,275
Twenty-one promised for you, sir.

505
00:33:56,276 --> 00:33:59,778
Twenty-three for Conway.
Two deadlocked. Four unknown.

506
00:33:59,779 --> 00:34:02,406
But I think things will
shift once they caucus.

507
00:34:02,407 --> 00:34:04,609
- All right, let's bring him in.
- Yes, sir.

508
00:34:12,875 --> 00:34:15,119
- Have a seat.
- I won't be long.

509
00:34:15,961 --> 00:34:19,790
Well, then, stand like a man who
doesn't know whether to sit or not.

510
00:34:20,258 --> 00:34:23,092
Congressman Romero, do
you have good news for me?

511
00:34:23,093 --> 00:34:26,471
Have your progressives seen the light?

512
00:34:26,472 --> 00:34:29,634
I did what I could, Mr. President.
I couldn't sway the bloc.

513
00:34:34,397 --> 00:34:37,816
Couldn't... or wouldn't?

514
00:34:37,817 --> 00:34:39,769
Maine and New Hampshire
are noncommittal.

515
00:34:40,778 --> 00:34:43,438
Congressman Wolcott is
turning Wisconsin for Conway.

516
00:34:49,370 --> 00:34:51,446
Tell me a different truth.

517
00:34:52,540 --> 00:34:54,291
They aren't listening to me.

518
00:34:54,292 --> 00:34:56,668
They believe supporting
you would do more damage

519
00:34:56,669 --> 00:35:00,122
to the Democratic Party and the
country than a Conway presidency.

520
00:35:03,968 --> 00:35:07,046
Well, I am sorry that
it has come to this.

521
00:35:09,390 --> 00:35:12,100
As am I. I'm sure we'll meet again, sir.

522
00:35:12,101 --> 00:35:16,396
Yes, but before you go, just
take a look around this room.

523
00:35:16,397 --> 00:35:20,309
Study every detail, every aspect.

524
00:35:21,361 --> 00:35:26,691
Because this is the last time
you will ever step foot in here.

525
00:35:27,867 --> 00:35:30,243
I'm sorry I couldn't be of more help.

526
00:35:30,244 --> 00:35:32,621
And to think you could
have made yourself memorable

527
00:35:32,622 --> 00:35:34,657
in so many other ways.

528
00:35:43,966 --> 00:35:46,343
I'll make sure he never
sees another committee, sir.

529
00:35:46,344 --> 00:35:50,597
Oh, no. He wants us to put up
a fight, make him feel bigger.

530
00:35:50,598 --> 00:35:53,099
He's just read some polls
and thinks I'm in trouble.

531
00:35:53,100 --> 00:35:57,396
That's why he's coming after
me. All right, we have 48 hours.

532
00:35:57,397 --> 00:36:00,023
I have to work Colorado
and shore up Tennessee.

533
00:36:00,024 --> 00:36:02,415
But Maine, Wisconsin and New Hampshire

534
00:36:02,416 --> 00:36:05,862
must be turned by any means necessary.

535
00:36:05,863 --> 00:36:08,365
All caucusing will end
by noon on Thursday,

536
00:36:08,366 --> 00:36:10,874
and then the vote in both
the House and the Senate

537
00:36:10,875 --> 00:36:12,744
is scheduled for 2:00.

538
00:36:12,745 --> 00:36:16,156
Well... I don't make the
decisions, Chuck. I just relay them.

539
00:36:17,542 --> 00:36:21,458
No. No, there will not be
a statement at this time.

540
00:36:21,459 --> 00:36:24,437
Well, because there's nothing
to state until after the vote.

541
00:36:24,438 --> 00:36:26,098
Close the door.

542
00:36:26,801 --> 00:36:29,323
Right. Yes.

543
00:36:30,679 --> 00:36:31,922
Yes.

544
00:36:32,640 --> 00:36:35,509
Yeah, please don't make
me say yes again. Mmm-hmm.

545
00:36:36,561 --> 00:36:39,388
Crazy days in these United States.

546
00:36:41,973 --> 00:36:43,720
So you wanna tell me
what was so important

547
00:36:43,721 --> 00:36:45,323
I had to get you through the gates?

548
00:36:45,324 --> 00:36:47,960
Girl named Lisa Williams came in.

549
00:36:48,238 --> 00:36:50,699
Says she was Rachel Posner's girlfriend.

550
00:36:50,700 --> 00:36:53,804
Said Doug Stamper knew Rachel.

551
00:36:54,078 --> 00:36:56,455
Feels like maybe he was
involved in her disappearance.

552
00:36:56,456 --> 00:36:58,665
And she said the words "Doug Stamper"?

553
00:36:58,666 --> 00:37:00,459
Rachel Posner went
missing around the time

554
00:37:00,460 --> 00:37:02,461
Stamper was carjacked and
had his head bashed in.

555
00:37:02,462 --> 00:37:05,122
And this Lisa thinks...

556
00:37:05,965 --> 00:37:07,875
Well, she's not sure exactly.

557
00:37:08,634 --> 00:37:11,595
- Well, can she prove a connection?
- It's all instinct at this point.

558
00:37:11,596 --> 00:37:13,597
Come on.

559
00:37:13,598 --> 00:37:15,036
You want a tour of the White House,

560
00:37:15,037 --> 00:37:16,908
you could just straight
up ask me for one.

561
00:37:19,144 --> 00:37:21,312
- Hammerschmidt.
- Is he making anything of it?

562
00:37:21,313 --> 00:37:25,017
Not yet. But the information about
her is on the top of his desk.

563
00:37:25,485 --> 00:37:26,769
Lisa Williams.

564
00:37:28,404 --> 00:37:30,154
Mr. Grayson, you have
a call on line three.

565
00:37:30,155 --> 00:37:31,948
Well, I gotta get back to it.

566
00:37:31,949 --> 00:37:34,151
If Tom follows up on her, let me know.

567
00:37:34,869 --> 00:37:37,947
- Thanks for the pass.
- It's temporary.

568
00:37:42,793 --> 00:37:44,119
Okay.

569
00:37:54,972 --> 00:37:56,841
LeAnn Harvey.

570
00:37:56,842 --> 00:37:58,975
Always burning the midnight oil.

571
00:37:58,976 --> 00:38:01,762
- Aidan?
- You ready to join me?

572
00:38:02,480 --> 00:38:06,274
Do you have any idea how much
jeopardy you have put me in?

573
00:38:06,275 --> 00:38:08,359
You should've gotten out
when you had the chance.

574
00:38:08,360 --> 00:38:10,444
You think you're out?

575
00:38:10,445 --> 00:38:13,823
- Listen, I need you to do me a favor.
- Where are you?

576
00:38:13,824 --> 00:38:18,787
Get the FBI, CIA, and whoever else
is looking for me to stop, now.

577
00:38:18,788 --> 00:38:20,789
Okay, Aidan, you know, just...

578
00:38:20,790 --> 00:38:23,374
come back here, and we will
figure this out together.

579
00:38:23,375 --> 00:38:24,798
If they keep coming after me,

580
00:38:24,799 --> 00:38:28,466
I will start leaking
things about the Underwoods.

581
00:38:28,506 --> 00:38:29,999
You don't wanna do that.

582
00:38:30,000 --> 00:38:33,466
You have no idea who
you're working for, LeAnn.

583
00:38:34,554 --> 00:38:35,963
God damn it.

584
00:38:37,097 --> 00:38:38,507
You have an update for the president?

585
00:38:39,308 --> 00:38:42,351
I've been juggling
senators most of the day.

586
00:38:42,352 --> 00:38:43,812
And your congressman from Maine?

587
00:38:43,813 --> 00:38:47,190
Is still my friend's father
and is still too busy to meet.

588
00:38:47,191 --> 00:38:49,859
So was Wisconsin and New
Hampshire, until I forced them to.

589
00:38:49,860 --> 00:38:51,695
You have your style, I have mine.

590
00:38:51,696 --> 00:38:53,572
The president doesn't
care about style, LeAnn.

591
00:38:53,573 --> 00:38:56,316
Just get the congressman
by the vote on Thursday.

592
00:39:11,591 --> 00:39:13,550
Will just walked in.

593
00:39:13,551 --> 00:39:14,752
Yeah.

594
00:39:16,220 --> 00:39:17,887
Okay.

595
00:39:17,888 --> 00:39:20,007
Talk soon. Bye.

596
00:39:25,062 --> 00:39:28,565
- That your mom?
- Yeah. She sends her best.

597
00:39:28,566 --> 00:39:30,017
Yeah, okay.

598
00:39:31,068 --> 00:39:33,854
- Where's Charlie?
- In the living room.

599
00:39:36,866 --> 00:39:40,076
Lily's playing in the den.

600
00:39:40,077 --> 00:39:41,828
- Where are you going?
- I should change.

601
00:39:41,829 --> 00:39:44,865
- You keep walking away from me, Hannah.
- Oh, please.

602
00:39:46,041 --> 00:39:47,659
Why would you invite him here?

603
00:39:48,460 --> 00:39:49,878
Now?

604
00:39:49,879 --> 00:39:52,130
He's a friend. He's your friend.

605
00:39:52,131 --> 00:39:54,561
And I thought maybe...

606
00:39:54,562 --> 00:39:57,468
it'd be something that you'd
be interested in looking at.

607
00:39:57,469 --> 00:40:00,096
I'm doing all I can.

608
00:40:00,097 --> 00:40:01,305
- All you can?
- Yes.

609
00:40:01,306 --> 00:40:02,389
Like doubt me?

610
00:40:02,390 --> 00:40:04,282
Like think that you know better
what's in my mind than I do?

611
00:40:04,283 --> 00:40:06,028
You know, you're the one who told Mark.

612
00:40:06,029 --> 00:40:07,771
I had to deny I had
anything to do with it.

613
00:40:07,772 --> 00:40:12,108
See, these people, Hannah...
Usher and Marshall...

614
00:40:12,109 --> 00:40:15,770
they're hired hands.
They can't be trusted.

615
00:40:16,739 --> 00:40:18,239
And Brockhart, he's in it for himself.

616
00:40:18,240 --> 00:40:22,451
But... but you, my darling, my love...

617
00:40:22,452 --> 00:40:24,613
- Stop it.
- Stop what?

618
00:40:25,330 --> 00:40:28,124
What are you gonna do? Take
the kids and go to your mom?

619
00:40:28,125 --> 00:40:30,619
As if you would.

620
00:40:31,461 --> 00:40:33,797
You already picked out
the White House china.

621
00:40:33,798 --> 00:40:37,667
You want this more than
anything. To be president.

622
00:40:39,011 --> 00:40:42,256
Don't you dare judge me
now that I want it, too.

623
00:40:50,981 --> 00:40:54,559
Yes, Congressman, but
flights are considered bribes.

624
00:40:55,986 --> 00:40:59,405
Yes, Congressman, it's not
your DUIs that concern us,

625
00:40:59,406 --> 00:41:01,191
it's your daughter's.

626
00:41:02,076 --> 00:41:05,721
Well, you should've done a
better job hiding the payouts.

627
00:41:05,722 --> 00:41:07,789
I mean, really.

628
00:41:07,790 --> 00:41:12,494
Yes, I'm sure you are...
but how old was he again?

629
00:41:13,420 --> 00:41:16,248
Yes, and he was in the
choir, is that right?

630
00:41:17,507 --> 00:41:22,087
But you can never erase an e-mail,
even my dead aunt knows that.

631
00:41:22,805 --> 00:41:25,007
Well, get back to me as soon as you can.

632
00:41:26,350 --> 00:41:29,511
- So?
- Maine finally looks solid, sir.

633
00:41:30,520 --> 00:41:32,306
New Hampshire doesn't, though.

634
00:41:33,440 --> 00:41:35,347
I've been sitting in here for hours

635
00:41:35,348 --> 00:41:37,276
busting my ass, getting some results.

636
00:41:37,277 --> 00:41:40,697
We should have secured New
Hampshire. How did LeAnn get Maine?

637
00:41:40,698 --> 00:41:42,365
She cashed in a favor.

638
00:41:42,366 --> 00:41:44,533
Well, why couldn't you
do the same goddamn thing?

639
00:41:44,534 --> 00:41:46,577
It doesn't make sense.

640
00:41:46,578 --> 00:41:49,448
I thought I had him. I mean...

641
00:41:50,165 --> 00:41:52,291
And it's not just New Hampshire.
Wisconsin should've folded.

642
00:41:52,292 --> 00:41:53,827
Should have?

643
00:41:54,544 --> 00:41:57,756
What's going on, Doug? What's happening?

644
00:41:57,757 --> 00:42:02,593
Is there some issue with
your commitment? Your loyalty?

645
00:42:02,594 --> 00:42:03,677
What?

646
00:42:03,678 --> 00:42:06,423
Perhaps you just aren't
worthy of my trust.

647
00:42:06,807 --> 00:42:08,767
I have given everything to you.

648
00:42:08,768 --> 00:42:12,270
Almost the entirety of my
professional life, Frank.

649
00:42:12,271 --> 00:42:14,514
You watch your tone.

650
00:42:16,483 --> 00:42:18,690
The things I've done...

651
00:42:18,903 --> 00:42:20,611
the things that I have
done to help keep you here,

652
00:42:20,612 --> 00:42:22,772
and you're gonna question my loyalty?

653
00:42:24,283 --> 00:42:27,994
Well, if we can't win, we have to
make sure that Conway doesn't either.

654
00:42:27,995 --> 00:42:31,497
Maine coming over only
means he won't get to 26.

655
00:42:31,498 --> 00:42:33,908
A deadlock isn't a win. But at least...

656
00:42:36,295 --> 00:42:38,455
I'm sorry, Doug. I was out of line.

657
00:42:42,009 --> 00:42:43,502
Let's get back to work.

658
00:42:52,686 --> 00:42:55,146
It's not just what he
did with all the data.

659
00:42:55,147 --> 00:42:57,273
Aidan had access to everything.

660
00:42:57,274 --> 00:42:59,733
We need to get the CIA
and FBI off his trail,

661
00:42:59,734 --> 00:43:02,236
- and I need your help.
- You're causing problems, LeAnn,

662
00:43:02,237 --> 00:43:04,648
which is the last thing
that we can afford right now.

663
00:43:05,407 --> 00:43:08,742
- Macallan sealed his own fate.
- I delivered Maine, Doug,

664
00:43:08,743 --> 00:43:11,196
which is more than you can
say about New Hampshire.

665
00:43:16,877 --> 00:43:18,078
I'll owe you.

666
00:43:19,171 --> 00:43:20,497
Not enough.

667
00:43:21,340 --> 00:43:24,884
Has the president spoken
to you about Aidan at all?

668
00:43:24,885 --> 00:43:26,086
No.

669
00:43:27,930 --> 00:43:29,763
Is he on a kill list?

670
00:43:29,764 --> 00:43:32,717
The United States of America
doesn't have kill lists, LeAnn.

671
00:43:35,729 --> 00:43:38,564
I am the only thing preventing
Mac from releasing what he has.

672
00:43:38,565 --> 00:43:42,311
And my guess is, whatever it
is, it'll implicate you, too.

673
00:43:43,528 --> 00:43:45,355
Am I wrong, Doug?

674
00:44:03,382 --> 00:44:06,134
Someone close to Senator
Mitchell woke me up with the news.

675
00:44:06,135 --> 00:44:07,593
Get me the vice president on the phone.

676
00:44:07,594 --> 00:44:08,719
That is not going to help.

677
00:44:08,720 --> 00:44:10,922
Well, how do you intend
on dealing with it?

678
00:44:22,317 --> 00:44:23,727
Donald.

679
00:44:25,654 --> 00:44:27,655
- Don't do it.
- Claire...

680
00:44:27,656 --> 00:44:30,658
I know what you and Senator
Mitchell were planning.

681
00:44:30,659 --> 00:44:32,535
Do what you were supposed to do.

682
00:44:32,536 --> 00:44:35,872
Move the anti-filibuster measure
forward so the Senate can vote

683
00:44:35,873 --> 00:44:38,124
- and elect me vice president.
- No.

684
00:44:38,125 --> 00:44:40,251
- No?
- All voices should be heard

685
00:44:40,252 --> 00:44:42,545
out of respect for the American people

686
00:44:42,546 --> 00:44:44,797
and the gravity of the vote
before the Senate today,

687
00:44:44,798 --> 00:44:47,550
the first like this since 1825.

688
00:44:47,551 --> 00:44:50,178
The House is about to vote,
Donald. What is going on?

689
00:44:50,179 --> 00:44:52,464
Let's get this done so you can go home.

690
00:44:53,765 --> 00:44:55,683
It looks like Conway is going to win.

691
00:44:55,684 --> 00:44:57,599
And no matter what people have told you,

692
00:44:57,600 --> 00:44:59,270
the Senate is voting for Brockhart.

693
00:44:59,271 --> 00:45:01,772
- It's country over party, Claire.
- Are you flexing?

694
00:45:01,773 --> 00:45:04,733
I'm stalling until
the House vote happens

695
00:45:04,734 --> 00:45:06,735
and then the Senate votes accordingly.

696
00:45:06,736 --> 00:45:08,217
You'd do that to the party

697
00:45:08,218 --> 00:45:09,863
to which you've devoted
your entire career?

698
00:45:09,864 --> 00:45:12,076
The country needs
clarity and resolution.

699
00:45:12,077 --> 00:45:13,534
That's the most important thing.

700
00:45:13,535 --> 00:45:16,037
There'll be another
election in four years.

701
00:45:16,038 --> 00:45:17,913
But look...

702
00:45:17,914 --> 00:45:20,666
I saw you when Francis was in the coma.

703
00:45:20,667 --> 00:45:24,413
What you're capable of
when given the opportunity.

704
00:45:25,130 --> 00:45:29,793
You still have a shot down
the road. I'd vote for you.

705
00:45:31,345 --> 00:45:33,512
Whatever you think, I
know I have the numbers.

706
00:45:33,513 --> 00:45:35,089
I don't think so.

707
00:45:36,558 --> 00:45:37,884
But come on.

708
00:45:40,312 --> 00:45:43,473
You're not like him. Be patient.

709
00:45:46,360 --> 00:45:49,487
You're a fool, Donald. You always were.

710
00:45:49,488 --> 00:45:53,866
You and your dumb, dead wife that
you never, ever shut up about.

711
00:45:53,867 --> 00:45:56,285
Principled? Idealistic?

712
00:45:56,286 --> 00:45:59,614
A champion for the people?
What did you ever actually do?

713
00:45:59,999 --> 00:46:01,749
Nothing.

714
00:46:01,750 --> 00:46:05,169
The great crusader? I don't think so.

715
00:46:05,170 --> 00:46:08,957
You just liked the position of
the bumbling idiot that you are.

716
00:46:10,217 --> 00:46:12,801
The optimist. The idealist.

717
00:46:12,802 --> 00:46:15,638
You loved it so much that
you couldn't even make a deal

718
00:46:15,639 --> 00:46:17,681
or move a single thing forward.

719
00:46:17,682 --> 00:46:20,219
You have a legacy of nothing.

720
00:46:20,935 --> 00:46:23,771
Sign the measure, get the
Republicans off the floor,

721
00:46:23,772 --> 00:46:25,765
start the vote now.

722
00:46:28,443 --> 00:46:30,479
Do you know what I just realized?

723
00:46:31,571 --> 00:46:32,981
Your initials...

724
00:46:33,948 --> 00:46:36,610
they're missing an "n" and a "t".

725
00:47:12,904 --> 00:47:15,231
The vote is inconclusive, Mr. President.

726
00:47:15,949 --> 00:47:18,909
Neither you nor Conway to 26.

727
00:47:18,910 --> 00:47:21,780
Still alive. What about Claire?

728
00:47:23,123 --> 00:47:26,292
This is impossible.

729
00:47:26,293 --> 00:47:28,877
I'm as surprised as you are.

730
00:47:28,878 --> 00:47:31,706
Maine. There was no heads-up on that.

731
00:47:33,217 --> 00:47:35,968
Look, I know this is
frustrating. It should be over.

732
00:47:35,969 --> 00:47:38,087
I... I'm the president.

733
00:47:39,473 --> 00:47:41,508
I don't care what they say, I...

734
00:47:42,226 --> 00:47:44,602
I'm fucking President
of the United States.

735
00:47:44,603 --> 00:47:45,845
No, you're not.

736
00:47:46,230 --> 00:47:48,021
The vice president
will be acting president

737
00:47:48,022 --> 00:47:50,600
for as long as it takes
the House to decide.

738
00:47:53,027 --> 00:47:54,646
I should make some calls.

739
00:48:41,803 --> 00:48:46,472
<font color="#007700">Synced and corrected by Aaronnmb</font>
